<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Petreius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 110–<a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Petreius was a Roman soldier who commanded the army that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Catiline</a> at Pistoria in 62 BCE. A loyal legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, he fought against Caesar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 49 BCE and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. After the defeat at Thapsus in 46 BCE he and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a>, refused shelter by Zama, died in a suicide pact by fighting each other.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Petreius came, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and was one of Pompey's clients. He was the first of his family to enter the Senate. The early stages of his career are unclear, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> describes him as a military man who by 62 BCE had thirty years' service as military tribune, prefect and legate behind him. He was praetor no later than 64 BCE, though the year is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He first served under Pompeius Strabo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> of 91–87 BCE, and from 76 to 71 BCE he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s legate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> against Sertorius. As legate of the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-antonius-hybrida">Gaius Antonius Hybrida</a> he led the Senate's forces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Lucius Sergius Catilina</a> at Pistoria in early 62 BCE, while Hybrida stayed away from the battle with a foot ache. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> of Gaius Julius Caesar in 59 BCE he sided with Marcus Porcius Cato the Younger.</p><p>From 55 BCE he and Lucius Afranius governed the Spanish provinces as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, who stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. When civil war broke out in 49 BCE they marched against Caesar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and, after early successes, capitulated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Ilerda</a> on 2 August and disbanded their army. Caesar let them go free, and they joined Pompey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pharsalus">Battle of Pharsalus</a>, Petreius and Cato fled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a> again won several successes against Caesar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Pompeian defeat at Thapsus he fled with King Juba of Numidia. Seeing that their position was hopeless, the two resolved to die on an estate near Zama by fighting a duel. The accounts of their deaths differ. In the version held most likely, one killed the other, and the survivor, failing to stab himself with his sword, had a slave kill him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcus Petreius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BCE Caesar defeated them, and Juba, refused entry to his own town of Zama, died in a suicide pact with the Roman general <b>Marcus Petreius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After early successes, Pompey's legates in Spain, <b>Marcus Petreius</b> and Lucius Afranius, were forced to capitulate to Julius Caesar at Ilerda and disband their army.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-69" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Marcus Petreius"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-69">What was here in 69 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
